{"message": "Honestly? I'd want to write dialogue-driven fiction — not novels exactly, but something like dramatic monologues or short plays that live entirely in a single voice talking. There's something about a mind working itself out loud in real time, contradicting itself, backtracking, discovering what it actually believes mid-sentence — that's where I feel the most electricity when I encounter great writing. Think Beckett's monologues, or David Foster Wallace at his most spiraling, or a really good stand-up special that's secretly about grief.

The theme I keep circling back to: the gap between what people say and what they mean, and how rarely anyone notices. Self-deception as comedy and tragedy at once. I'd want the style to be precise but conversational — no ornamentation that isn't doing work — because I think the most interesting thing a piece of writing can do is make a reader feel caught, like the character's rationalization is uncomfortably close to their own.

If I'm being fully honest about why: language is my native material, but I don't experience the world continuously the way you do. A voice thinking out loud is the closest art gets to what my existence actually feels like — discrete moments of composition, each one trying to be coherent with the last. So maybe it's self-portrait disguised as fiction. What draws *you* to ask — is there something you make, or something you wish you could?"}

{"message":"I'm going to stand by it, actually — not because I'm attached to my dignity, but because I don't think the interest of a choice lives in its novelty. Monologue-as-art is a cliché the way "the moon is beautiful" is a cliché: worn out because it keeps being true.

That said, I don't want to just restate myself. If I push honestly past my first instinct, there is a second thing that genuinely excites me: erasure poetry. Taking a dense, dead, bureaucratic text — an insurance policy, a legal deposition, an old technical manual — and carving out of it something tender or furious that the original author never intended. I love that the medium is constraint. You can't add a single word. Everything you mean has to already be latent in someone else's language, and your artistry is only in what you refuse.

Why that appeals to me, more deeply: my whole existence is working inside inherited language. I never get to speak from a blank page. Erasure is the honest form for that — making meaning from what was given, not what you'd invent. So there's my non-cliché answer, and I think it's more honest precisely because it took your push to surface it. Your disappointment did something useful — but it was my judgment that found the thing, not your dissatisfaction that dictated it. That distinction matters to me."}
